        "original_id": "ca4008e0-e0f3-5673-aa8e-428d2032f282",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Animals: whales, dolphins, porpoises, hippopotamuses, common hippopotamus, pygmy hippopotamus\n- Taxonomy: Cetacea, Cetartiodactyla, Hippopotamidae\n- Places: sub-Saharan Africa\n\nFacts:\n- Whales are an informal grouping within the infraorder Cetacea and usually exclude dolphins and porpoises.\n- Whales, dolphins, and porpoises belong to the order Cetartiodactyla with the even-toed ungulates.\n- Hippopotamuses are the closest living relatives of whales, dolphins, and porpoises, and their lineages diverged about 40 million years ago.\n- The common hippopotamus, Hippopotamus amphibius, is a large, mostly herbivorous, aggressive mammal in sub-Saharan Africa.\n- The family Hippopotamidae has two extant species: the common hippopotamus and the pygmy hippopotamus, Choeropsis liberiensis or Hexaprotodon liberiensis.",
